{
 "metadata": {
  "note": "Verbatim DDInter 2.0 slice for issue #185 - the restating-existing-therapy skip in the duplicate-therapy (class) arm. Rows are field-for-field their KB rows in KB order, and the interaction rows are every KB pair among these drugs. TWO slices, one per route into the defect. (1) The PPIs: the KB's Omeprazole row publishes rxnorm_name 'esomeprazole' and the single ATC code A02BC05, which is esomeprazole's; omeprazole's own A02BC01 is absent from the whole 19 MB KB (measured 2026-08-12 through DrugReference.normalizedAtcCodes over DdiDrugReferenceSource.parse), so an omeprazole order on a dictionary that maps the concept correctly carries a code the KB's Omeprazole row does not - the two code sets are disjoint while their level-4 subgroups still meet. Esomeprazole keeps the fix honest: same rxnorm_name family, same ATC code, a different drugbank_id and so a different DrugReference.substanceKey, so a chip about it against an omeprazole order is a genuine finding and must survive. Pantoprazole is the third A02BC substance and the only one of the three the KB rates against Omeprazole. (2) Isoniazid and Rifapentine: the 3.7.1 demo dictionary's own 'Isoniazid / Rifapentine' concept maps to J04AB05 (rifapentine) and J04AC51 (the combination code, absent KB-wide), so the partner RESOLVES - to Rifapentine, by the code - and it is the ORDER'S NAME that names isoniazid. That is the route a skip keyed on the resolved entry alone would still miss."
 },
